a former Minneapolis police officer  could face more than 15 years in prison  for shooting and killing an unarmed yoga  teacher Mohammed nor was found guilty  yesterday of murdering Justine Damon nor  was responding to Damon's 9-1-1 call  nearly two years ago when he opened fire  Dean Reynolds is outside the courthouse  in Minneapolis where the verdict was  handed down Dean good morning good  morning the jury reached a decision  after deliberating for roughly 11 hours  finding Neuer guilty of murder in the  third degree and manslaughter in the  second degree but acquitting him of the  more serious charge of murder in the  second degree this welcome conviction  will not bring Justine back however we  celebrate that some form of justice has  occurred this week friends and family of  Justine Damon breed the sigh of relief  following Tuesday's guilty verdict  against former Minneapolis police  officer Muhammad nor we believe he was  properly charged with a crime while  Damon's father said he was satisfied  with the outcome  Damon's fiance criticized the  Minneapolis Police Department but that  night there was a tragic lapse of care  and complete disregard for the sanctity  of life on July 15 2017 newer and his  partner were responding to Damon's 9-1-1  call about a possible sexual assault  near her home while searching for a  suspect from inside their patrol car  Nour testified that he heard a loud slap  on the driver's side of the vehicle and  shot daymond from the passenger seat out  of fear for his partner's life the  month-long trial hinged on whether Knorr  use of deadly force was justified  Minnesota law allows officers to use  deadly force if they perceive a threat  but defense attorneys could not convince  the jury Damon's alleged slap justified  the shooting well the evidence showed  that the officer acted unreasonably  Hennepin County Attorney Mike Freeman  says nor is the first Minnesota police  officer to be convicted of murdering  someone while on duty it does not give  us pleasure to call out police  is wrongdoing but when it occurs it is  our job to let the public know and in  extreme cases to bring charges and  prosecution  now the police and the police union  leaders say they respect Tuesday's  verdict the judge in the case denied  bond to nor who is due back in court for  sentencing on June 7th so far no word on  whether his defense attorneys plan to  appeal Gale alright thank you very much  Dean
 

